`logger.outro('')` with an empty string is the only such call in the entire codebase — every other `logger.outro` call carries a meaningful status message (e.g., `'Storybook build completed successfully'` or `'Storybook exited with an error'` in `core.ts`). In clack TUI mode, passing an empty string renders a blank closing box (`└───`) after the step/error line, which is visually noisy without adding information. Consider consolidating the status text into `outro` directly and removing the redundant `logger.step`/`logger.error` calls:

```ts
if (problems.length > 0) {
  logger.log(problems.map((p) => p.stack).join('\n'));
  logger.outro(CLI_COLORS.error('Smoke tests failed.'));
} else {
  logger.outro(CLI_COLORS.success('Smoke tests passed, exiting.'));
}
```

This matches the established pattern and uses `outro` for what it is semantically meant for: the final exit message.
